the two ukrainian journalists have just  been named as the recipients of dw's  freedom of speech award for 2022. video  journalist mr slav chernov and  photographer jenny malaletka of the  associated press spent 20 days in  mariupol under constant bombardment the  last international journalists left  mariupol they risked their lives daily  to record and send out footage  documenting the war in a city cut off  from the outside world the dw freedom of  speech award honours  honours people who have been have done  outstanding in their efforts to promote  freedom of expression and report the  truth a warning to our viewers this  report does include some distressing  images and we won't have video  statements from the laureates for safety  reasons they didn't want to be  interviewed on camera  a city's agony that's how mrs lavchernov  and evgeny maloletka described what they  witnessed in mario paul  arrived in the strategic port one hour  before the war broke out early on  february 24th  people were soon dying all around  doctors battled to save children  newborns and adults often without  success  hakeev-born video journalist mitis  levchernov has covered wars in iraq  syria and many more countries working  for the associated press  photographer evgeny maruletka is also  from ukraine's east from beriansk both  have won multiple awards for their work  as russian bombs rained down on mariopol  the telephone radio and television  towers were destroyed  with no phone signal people couldn't  even call an ambulance doctors begged  the journalists to film families  bringing in their own dead and wounded  so the world would know what was  happening  many of the bodies had to be buried in  mass graves that galvanized the  journalists to take ever greater risks  with no pictures of demolished buildings  and dying children says mr slav chernov  the russian forces could do whatever  they wanted if not for us there would be  nothing i have never felt that breaking  the silence was so important  the journalist footage of a russian  attack on a maternity clinic on march  9th was a turning point  the images of traumatized families being  rescued by ukrainian soldiers prompted  an international outcry over russia's  disregard for civilian life  this pregnant woman carried out on a  stretcher later died  along with her child  moscow claims the whole thing was staged  with actors  and the airstrike never took place  russia now broadcasts its propaganda  over radio and mario paul accusing  ukrainians of holding the city hostage  and shooting at buildings  with russian forces searching for the  two journalists they managed to escape  mariopol on march 15th  with the help of the ukrainian army  we were told that if we were captured by  the russians they would force us to say  what they wanted us to say into the  camera evgeny malaletka told dw  i didn't want to experience firsthand  how the russian intelligence services  deal with people who are detained  the pair continue to report on the war  in ukraine from elsewhere in the country  dw's director general peter limberg is  in the studio with me mr limburg welcome  uh there are so many journalists  reporting in ukraine why what  distinguishes these two and how hard is  it to choose them  everyone has to say that there are  really a lot of great and really brave  war reporters in ukraine and uh rebecca  you were among them you were in kiev you  just returned so  um but i think uh  the two um channel and madoletka they  they did something special being being  in a place where nobody else was and  they  they didn't leave it  they just stayed there because because  they  knew as we saw also in the report that  if they don't bring light into this uh  nobody nobody will care  and so i think this is an amazing brave  attitude towards our profession and i  think this is uh why they deserve this  award  yeah i was just rereading their story uh  yesterday they wrote something for the  associated press afterwards and then  they were actually forcibly evacuated by  the ukrainians because their life was at  such risk i mean it's an incredible  story um what message are you or dw  trying to send to other journalists with  this year's prize  i think it's also recognition for war  reporters and reporters who  go into dangerous places because we all  know it journalism has become much more  dangerous in the past years but  this war is another  example for this and all the wars which  we have been covering uh whether it's  syria or whether it's uh some wards  far away from europe shows us that you  have to have free press on the ground  otherwise people do a horrendous things  and  so i think this is why we say that it's  it's important that we have these  journalists when they are well educated  and trained i mean we don't send people  into crisis situations who have not  special training and we do everything to  protect them but i think it's absolutely  vital to be on the ground  you mentioned press freedom there and i  wanted to touch on  russia the russian government cracking  down on free media  deutsche was among those forced out of  the country earlier this year um how do  you see  those developments in russia  oh we see russia turning into a  kind of fascist  rule  where freedom of press is erased totally  and it's pure propaganda coming out of  every russian outlet  so it is a situation where i think that  journalism is is very hard to do  and we all see that there are a lot of  russians going into exile russian  journalists and we see  that we are opening our bureau now in  riga  in latvia so that they have more access  and be near  to russia but uh it's getting uh  difficult and i think for the coming  month and years it will not get better  well then i'll end on coming back to our  prize winners because this is really  about them how difficult was it to  choose those two particular  men  i think  it wasn't so difficult because it is  really something special outstanding  they changed history because people  could see what's going on for the first  time and they saw the brutality of  what's going on and so i think uh it  wasn't so difficult we chose the right  ones i think  peter limberg thank you very much for  your time welcome rebecca thank you
 

